WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:27.280
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: The corporate part of this
report normally covers our services, what

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.760
we are 
doing inside NHS England, and our major change

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.719
programmes. I have nothing to bring to the
Board’s attention on our services, but I

00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:42.440
did just want to assure you that we are continuing
to make progress on improving many aspects

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.850
of how NHS England operates; its core processes,
recruitment, procurement, its governance,

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:56.179
and aspects that have been raised in the Audit
Committee, and our self-engagement, which

00:00:56.179 --> 00:01:01.389
is really important. We have just had the
results of our staff survey which indicate

00:01:01.389 --> 00:01:08.689
an improvement there of 4%, which I think
is really positive, but more of that probably

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:10.089
another time.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:17.710
Let me say something about the national programmes.
This report reflects the work that we have

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:24.960
been doing to do a mid-year stock-take on
the progress and delivery confidence of all

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.500
of our national corporate priority programmes;
these are the ten that we set out in our business

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:39.149
plan last year. I am pleased that most of
those are making very good progress, and that

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:45.159
is particularly important as most of them
get closer to or actually in the stage of

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:51.450
delivering real change on the ground, such
as Cally was describing earlier with cancer.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:57.400
That is very positive. Two of those programmes
we recently discussed at our executive group:

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.510
learning disabilities and self-care, where
we identified and agreed some further work

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:10.460
that needs to be done to move them in a more
positive way.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:18.730
The other thing that I did want to refer to
was primary care services, which we discussed

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:29.000
in some detail at the last Board meeting.
Again, we are continuing to focus – I personally,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.410
and many of my team are continuing to focus
heavily – on ensuring that we are putting

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:39.640
all of the right effort in and working with
Capita and holding them to account for improving

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:47.360
the services that they are delivering. I am
pleased that progress is being made. It is

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:55.460
always slower to get out of a crisis than
you would want it to be, but I am positive

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.370
about the moves that are being made and about
the contribution that Capita are making, and

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:05.650
their commitment to resourcing the services
in the way that they do need to be resourced,

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:13.560
so that is positive. I talked last time about
a plan and timescales, which we communicated

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:20.870
with stakeholders and the Board, about the
recovery timetable, and we are largely sticking

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:29.220
to that timetable and plan. Improvements are
being made week on week, literally, and I

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:38.650
am anticipating that most of the services
will be largely fully recovered by March,

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:44.010
with staging posts in between, where we make
good progress.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:49.750
You referred earlier to the performers lists
and the dental performers list in particular.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:57.650
Performers lists continue to be a challenging
area, but one where we and our medical teams

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:03.180
in particular, and our local primary care
teams, are investing a huge amount of time

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:10.810
to ensure that GPs, dental performers and
optom performers all get on the performers

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:15.840
lists in the timescales that we need them
to. A lot of work to do, still, a lot of risk

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:22.130
still there, but I think we are making adequate
progress.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.600
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I have to say, I am reassured
by what Karen has just said about primary

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:37.080
care support. We were pretty disappointed
at the progress on that when you reported

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:42.720
earlier in the year, so I think we can take
some heart from what we have just heard about

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.290
the improvements that we are seeing, but I
know you will not hesitate to alert us should

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:52.860
there be any falling away from our expectations
over the coming weeks and months.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:58.500
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: It is in the report, but
I just wanted to highlight the consultation

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.010
that is out at the moment about the changes
to services at Mersey Care, so that is the

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:08.410
old Whalley site, Calderstones, the only standalone
learning disability hospital. I am just encouraging

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:14.600
everybody to respond, because that is really
important and a major step in what we are

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.040
planning to do with learning disability, where
we continue to reduce the number of inpatients

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.920
month on month.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.500
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I really just wanted
to talk about a couple of elements. I should

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:38.530
start really with winter A&amp;E and the pressures
in the system. We are now seeing that A&amp;E

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:50.820
performance has dropped to 89%, which is obviously
below the 95% target. This is a combination

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:58.110
of three things that we need to jointly address.
One of them is demand; as you will see in

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:05.830
the figures, attendance is up by 4.5%, admissions
are up by 2.7%, so we are seeing the growing

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:14.710
pressure on the front door of hospital systems.
Whilst the back door, delayed discharges rose

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:22.260
to 200,000 days in the month, so we are talking
about nearly 7,000 beds’ worth of capacity

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.480
being held up by delayed discharges. If we
had said at the beginning of the year we were

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.310
going to close seven large hospitals, people
would have thought that was a foolish decision,

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:38.880
so we are taking a lot of capacity out of
the system. Then there is always room for

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.840
efficiency within the system, of patients
being moved through more smoothly, getting

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.110
the care they need and then being moved on
to the right setting.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:54.010
The whole system is trying to work together;
we have primary and community care, social

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:59.730
care and the acute trusts together trying
to resolve the flow, which is why we are working

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:05.980
very closely and every day with NHS Improvement
to say that this is not a competition about

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:11.669
who has the bigger problem; this is about
how we align in order to optimise for the

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:19.940
patients. I think the public can help us and
help themselves a bit here, as well, because

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:25.500
the first question is to say: do not go to
A&amp;E unless you really need to; we have a set

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:32.510
of other services. The most appropriate place
for someone to go who needs advice is usually

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:40.890
their GP, and so make a GP appointment or
ring 111 and speak to somebody who can give

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:47.850
you advice, or use one of our urgent care
centres, minor injuries units.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:55.540
I thought it might just be worth sharing with
the Board the facilities that are available.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:08:04.790
It is worth knowing that 1.3 million people
turned up to A&amp;E in October. 1.2 million people

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:14.020
rang 111, so the 111 service is taking as
many contacts now as A&amp;E did, and 650,000

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:19.520
people went to a walk-in service. The 650,000
people who went to a walk-in service, the

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:26.680
compliance with the four-hour standard for
those services was 99.4%, so if you go to

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:33.190
a walk-in service or a minor injuries unit,
the probability that you will be in and out

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:38.119
in a relatively short period of time is very,
very high, and the nearest service to us here

00:08:38.119 --> 00:08:43.899
would be at Guy’s Hospital down the road,
and they were running at 99.9% in October

00:08:43.899 --> 00:08:48.800
on their four-hour turnaround. There are other
services, and they are not small services;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.069
half as many people are going to a walk-in
service as are going to A&amp;E, and we also know

00:08:53.069 --> 00:08:59.079
that half of the people who go to an A&amp;E could
have received their services in a walk-in

00:08:59.079 --> 00:09:05.660
centre or from their GP, or by going to a
pharmacy. If you do not know where to go,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:12.930
ring 111 and they will help guide you there,
but if you need routine advice, you are better

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:18.110
off – if you want it now – going to a
walk-in centre in terms of your own time,

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:23.110
and also not filling up our A&amp;Es, when what
they need to do is focus on the people who

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:28.129
need A&amp;E care. If we can take the people away
from A&amp;Es who do not need to be there and

00:09:28.129 --> 00:09:32.980
provide services for them elsewhere, then
our A&amp;Es will run better and the people who

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:37.750
desperately need the care we provide in an
A&amp;E will get that more quickly and more effectively.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:43.960
The public can help the NHS here, and we think
we have the set of services to help them help

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.140
us, so I would encourage that.

00:09:46.149 --> 00:09:57.139
If I just move briefly on to elective waits,
the access to surgical services and the RTT

00:09:57.139 --> 00:10:07.730
standard, we are still sitting at over 90%
access within 18 weeks. That is exceptionally

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:12.759
good by international standards, but we are
seeing a pressure on access to elective services

00:10:12.759 --> 00:10:18.839
because of the pressure on beds from emergency
services. Day surgery is going up very quickly;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:23.940
inpatient surgery is not, so we have a risk
that when we come out of winter, we will have

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:29.740
seen waiting times go up because of the capacity
taken by emergency, so we are very aware of

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:30.820
that.

00:10:32.149 --> 00:10:37.490
The third and final thing I would just cover
is preparation for winter. Work has now gone

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:42.559
on across the country to ensure that we have
GP and community services cover through the

00:10:42.559 --> 00:10:48.809
bank holidays, so that the right services
are in place. These should be being advertised

00:10:48.809 --> 00:10:55.589
on local radio, in local newspapers. You can
find it by going to nhs.net if you want to

00:10:55.589 --> 00:11:02.649
know which GP practice you can get access
to on Boxing Day; if you go to our website,

00:11:02.649 --> 00:11:10.000
nhs.net, or ring 111, they will guide you
to the right services. We have made sure that

00:11:10.009 --> 00:11:15.969
people can get access to things that are not
an A&amp;E department during the bank holiday

00:11:15.969 --> 00:11:20.100
period.
We have also made some quite significant investments

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:29.319
over the last few weeks into primary care,
in order to strengthen primary care’s ability

00:11:29.319 --> 00:11:36.559
to cope, not just with winter, but generally.
Nearly £150 million has gone into primary

00:11:36.559 --> 00:11:47.069
care in improving investment in the most vulnerable
practices, strengthening the areas where there

00:11:47.069 --> 00:11:55.199
is most need. I think the pieces are in place,
but it is extremely busy; the hospitals are

00:11:55.199 --> 00:12:00.850
under a lot of pressure at the moment, whole
health systems are under pressure, and I think

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:02.150
people are doing the best that they can.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:08.780
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Just a tiny thing: Matthew,
would it be nhs.uk to go to?

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.310
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Sorry, did I say dot
net?

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:13.790
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Yes.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Okay, it is www.nhs.uk.

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:16.350
Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: A very good point, thank

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:23.740
you.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Matthew, I think it is really

00:12:23.749 --> 00:12:29.540
interesting, the comments you made about the
scale and the availability of services outside

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:34.240
of A&amp;E. What proportion of the population
do you think actually knows about them?

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:44.759
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I guess, given that more
people use 111 or a walk-in centre than use

00:12:44.759 --> 00:12:50.079
A&amp;E, somebody must know about it, because
we have over a&nbsp;million people using them.

00:12:50.079 --> 00:12:55.589
I think we have not always made it as simple
as we could to know which of these services

00:12:55.589 --> 00:12:59.420
you should call on, which is why we are trying
to develop the telephone service as the right

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:05.519
way of routing you to the care you need. First,
pick up the phone, make a call before you

00:13:05.519 --> 00:13:13.689
go somewhere, and we will try and make sure
that you get access to the right services.

00:13:13.689 --> 00:13:17.169
The A&amp;E brand is very strong; people know
what is there, they know to go there.

00:13:17.169 --> 00:13:23.149
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: My instinct is that it would
be worth us thinking hard about, whether it

00:13:23.149 --> 00:13:29.060
be social media, whether it be easy availability
– I have just been on to one to see where

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:36.649
they were by my own house; it is easy once
you find them. I think it would be really

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:41.670
worth thinking hard, because my hypothesis
would be that it is not as well known, especially

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:47.290
if it were linked to the point that you said,
which is about, ‘If you go here, your service

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:50.970
is X minutes; if you go there, it is Y hours.’
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think that is a very

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:58.639
fair point.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I am just curious: Turning

00:13:58.639 --> 00:14:06.790
Point provides a service to a lot of people
with complex needs; drugs, alcohol. I know

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:15.420
that over Christmas, the numbers of people
who are repeat A&amp;E users is significant in

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:20.480
some of our services, and I just wondered
– not necessarily for those people who get

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:25.249
that very important message that you just
set out, but the people at the real sharp

00:14:25.249 --> 00:14:31.220
end of the inverse care law who are in and
out of A&amp;E multiple times, sometimes in the

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:36.180
case of one day – what work we are doing
with organisations, not just Turning Point,

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:44.779
but other ones, where we have a high proportion
of highly complex, substance misuse, mental

00:14:44.779 --> 00:14:52.089
health or both, potentially homeless, but
are heavy users of A&amp;E? I am curious about

00:14:52.089 --> 00:14:56.850
the proportion of A&amp;E numbers that include
those people, and I am curious about what

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:02.029
partnerships we are or could be creating to
relieve the pressure on A&amp;E, particularly

00:15:02.029 --> 00:15:06.839
over this period, from that particular cohort.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think it is a really

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:11.519
good question. I do not have the numbers in
front of me, but I think what we do know is

00:15:11.519 --> 00:15:17.999
that those very complex patients, whilst in
headcount numbers they may not be enormous

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:24.860
numbers, in the amount of time they spend
in hospital and how difficult it is to discharge

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:31.629
them once they have been admitted, those are
very significant. Some A&amp;Es have brilliant

00:15:31.629 --> 00:15:36.149
arrangements with the equivalents of Turning
Point around the country to work in partnership

00:15:36.149 --> 00:15:40.920
with their mental health trusts, with the
voluntary sector, in order to provide the

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:46.369
right input at the right time to those communities,
and others less so, and particularly cities

00:15:46.369 --> 00:15:52.829
where it is a very transient population that
turn up in A&amp;E. It is part of what I would

00:15:52.829 --> 00:15:59.410
expect of the partnership around STPs to be
developing. That is why the conversation needs

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:03.509
to be wider now, because we need to start
to think about: who are the most vulnerable

00:16:03.509 --> 00:16:09.610
groups, who are the highest user groups, and
what is the right way of reaching them? It

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:15.410
is not always going to be done by the big
hospital in the middle of the city; frequently,

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:20.899
that is not the service that those people
need the most, if you intervene at the right

00:16:20.899 --> 00:16:22.339
time.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Do we have the numbers,

00:16:22.339 --> 00:16:25.359
by the way?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I am sure we do.

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:32.540
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I think people are often better
than we realise at navigating towards the

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:39.069
service that works for them, and that is one
of the reasons why, as A&amp;E gets quicker at

00:16:39.069 --> 00:16:44.889
processing people, and it gets easier to be
seen quickly in A&amp;E than it does in one’s

00:16:44.889 --> 00:16:51.399
GP, A&amp;Es attract people who could equally
well be treated in primary care. My experience

00:16:51.399 --> 00:16:57.999
with NHS Direct was that, when we launched
our Symptom Checker app, which was not promoted,

00:16:57.999 --> 00:17:02.730
a million people downloaded it within a matter
of weeks, because they found it, they told

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:09.610
people about it, it was useful, they grabbed
it. My question is: how close are we to getting

00:17:09.610 --> 00:17:14.770
to the point where, rather than what you described
just now – if you do not know what you want,

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:21.880
pick up the phone – we get to the point
of, if you do not know quite what it is that

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:26.899
you want, go online? Which is, after all,
how most people address most questions these

00:17:26.899 --> 00:17:32.889
days. How close are we to being at the point
of being able to offer people something really

00:17:32.889 --> 00:17:36.429
helpful for them online as a first point of
contact?

00:17:36.429 --> 00:17:43.649
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: That is a very good question,
because our wider approach to urgent and emergency

00:17:43.649 --> 00:17:48.370
care starts with an online presence, then
it has a telephone presence, and then it has

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:57.110
a set of places that you can go to. With the
honourable exception of what NHS Direct did

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:02.900
a number of years ago, that online presence
piece has been largely missing. We have been

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:07.610
doing a lot of work over the last few months,
and we would expect to be piloting in a number

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:14.330
of parts of the country an online experience
in the next couple of months, and what we

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:17.380
are planning on doing is looking at three
or four different ways of doing it, doing

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:24.480
a proper evaluation and saying, ‘Which of
these are the most effective? Which do the

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.210
public enjoy using? Which ones give the best
guidance to where you should go?’

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:36.279
The other part of that, though, is I think
the online service and the telephone service

00:18:36.279 --> 00:18:41.200
have to live very closely together, because
by definition your online service is going

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:47.409
to be quite risk-averse, so where are you
going to put somebody who you think there

00:18:47.409 --> 00:18:50.861
might be something wrong with? The right thing
to do is to get the clinician on whom you

00:18:50.861 --> 00:18:56.440
can talk to about it, so that there is a streaming-out
of, ‘Look, you just have a cold, you are

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:03.010
fine,’ but there is a whole middle area:
the technology is moving very quickly, but

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:11.000
at the moment, the technology is not confident
to just diagnose, or we as the NHS need to

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:15.059
do quite a lot of evaluation to be confident
that that is the right thing to do. It is

00:19:15.059 --> 00:19:23.080
getting it positioned in the right place.
In the next couple of months, we will be starting

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:29.880
to pilot that triaging and adjusting the balance
of who gets directed to a walk-in centre,

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:34.429
who gets directed to A&amp;E, who gets directed
to a phone call, and who gets told, ‘You

00:19:34.429 --> 00:19:36.840
can just go to a pharmacy and sort yourself
out.’

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:42.620
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: If I may say so, let us not
default to, ‘You need to go somewhere,’

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:49.830
wherever that is, because I think the experience
is, on many occasions, people just need advice

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:52.080
but they can look after themselves.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes.

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:56.080
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: That is a huge chunk, actually,
and of course, it is much better for people

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:59.680
if they are able to look after themselves
and not have to go anywhere at all.

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:03.620
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, I agree.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I was quite taken by the

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:08.360
figures that you gave about walk-in centres,
for example, and the impact that channel-shifting

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:15.340
has on the capacity of real A&amp;E departments
to deal with the four-hour expectations, because

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:22.019
of the change in acuity, and of course, numbers
of attendances are still continuing to rise.

00:20:22.019 --> 00:20:26.460
Just to reflect on that.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, and we have quite

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:36.610
a lot of data around that, including if you
give an elderly person advice on how to look

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:40.960
after themselves at home, you can keep them
in their home. If they turn up in an A&amp;E department

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:48.320
at 23.00, they are likely to get admitted,
so it is not a binary thing. Intervening at

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:53.950
the right time to help somebody stay in place
is a lot better than trying to send them away

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:56.429
from an A&amp;E department at 23.00, which tends
not to happen.

00:20:56.429 --> 00:20:59.109
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes, I think that is a very
good point.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:05.880
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: I just wanted to build on
some of the things that Matthew said around

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:10.460
how we are trying to get some of those messages
out over the winter. One of the things that

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:14.930
we have really done is concentrate very hard
on helping people to help themselves, for

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:18.860
the reasons that Joanne has just mentioned.
Particularly for the elderly, we know that

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:23.730
we have something like in excess of 25,000
additional deaths over the winter period,

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:28.509
and so some of that is about pre-empting and
actually taking action really quickly. Some

00:21:28.509 --> 00:21:33.200
of the messages we have been giving are things
like keeping warm and wearing different layers;

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:39.190
having your flu jab, if you are eligible for
that; keeping your house at a reasonable temperature

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:44.460
and providing advice on how to access financial
support to do that.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:48.649
In terms of getting additional support, there
are two really key things, I think. One is

00:21:48.649 --> 00:21:53.940
about going to see your clinical pharmacist,
your local pharmacy, very early if you feel

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:58.009
a bit unwell, because actually, those are
very highly trained clinical professionals;

00:21:58.009 --> 00:22:01.440
they have something like between four and
five years’ training, and actually can give

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:06.070
advice on whether you need to see a GP or
you need to be directed somewhere else.

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:09.250
Then the other point that Matthew has talked
about a lot, which I think is right, is about

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:15.760
dialling 111, and the reason for that is many
people, when they are faced with a problem,

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:21.650
do not remember what services are available
in various different urgent walk-in centres,

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:25.470
and they are not sure whether they need an
X-ray or whether they need to see a doctor,

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:31.460
and some of them are nurse-led very well.
Actually dialling 111 enables people to be

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:37.190
directed to the right service, and I think
if we can do those two things, that actually

00:22:37.190 --> 00:22:44.120
really helps people; clinical pharmacy or
111 really makes a difference, as does actually

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:50.010
trying to look after yourself at home but
getting help and getting support early, rather

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:54.139
than waiting until people become very unwell
and then end up in an emergency department

00:22:54.139 --> 00:22:57.769
and need admission.
Those are the real messages that we have been

00:22:57.769 --> 00:23:02.120
trying to give, as well as the really important
one around: if you have elderly relatives,

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:06.990
you have elderly neighbours, actually just
checking that they are okay; just checking

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:11.110
that they are okay and accessing support quickly.
Those sorts of messages are really meaningful,

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:15.549
and actually are really having quite a positive
impact on people out in the system who are

00:23:15.549 --> 00:23:18.720
recalling and remembering some of those messages
that we are putting out.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:23.880
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: It might be worth just
noting that Jane has been doing a lot of sofa-surfing

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:29.529
around the media of late, presenting the case
to the public, which I think is really powerful

00:23:29.529 --> 00:23:34.630
given that we now know that nurses are one
of the most trusted professions in the public’s

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:38.470
receipt of information like this. It is good
stuff.

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.850
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Just on that last point,
I guess that is the reason we now have Jane

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:42.400
rather than Bruce!

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.039
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Can I just say, thanks very

00:24:02.039 --> 00:24:07.890
much for that update. In terms of the forthcoming
winter period, which will be difficult across

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:16.629
the NHS, it is worth drawing everybody’s
attention to the website, www.nhs.uk/staywell,

00:24:16.629 --> 00:24:22.760
and on there is the performance by the Lewisham
and Greenwich NHS Hospital Choir which, I

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:29.740
have to say, put into even more poetic measures
the points that we have been making in the

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:33.830
discussion today, so I commend it to everybody.
Great performance, and thanks to our comms

00:24:33.830 --> 00:24:36.450
team for helping to organise that.

